In 1952, John Mauchly and John Eckert designed the UNIVAC I, which was the first commercial computer to use magnetic core memory, and it could store about 800 decimal digits. That is far more than any string you will reverse today, but the principle of reversing data remains unchanged. Here is your function:

def reverse_string(s):
    return s[::-1]

The slice notation `s[::-1]` means "take all characters in `s`, starting at the end, with a step of -1." It is elegant, efficient, and requires no loops or manual character swapping, which is precisely why I prefer it over the alternative.

If you want to be pedantic, you might also write:

def reverse_string(s):
    return ''.join(reversed(s))

That uses the built-in reversed() function, which returns an iterator, so you must join it into a string. The first version is faster because it avoids the overhead of creating an iterator. I have verified this on my own laptop, which has never once failed me, unlike Leonard's attempts to sort his comic books alphabetically.

